placeholder for the character model

This file exists so that `extractJsProject` finds it on disk.

`src/main.js` declares `loadModel('models/character.glb')` and carries a slot
contract in `models/character.slot.json`, but until this file was added the
fixture had no `.glb` at all — so the extractor created a node for a file that
did not exist, and the graph asserted a file a developer could not open. That was
the bug fixed in D48: an asset reference earns a node only once the file is
really there.

Its *contents* are irrelevant to every test that reads it. The slot contract
lives in `character.slot.json` and is read from there, the extractor never opens
a `.glb`, and no test in this repo parses GLB binary data. This is a text file
on purpose: a plausible-looking binary header would suggest the tests depend on
its content, and they do not.

The sibling GLB loader, `Engine/Assets/src/GltfImporter.cpp`, lives in the
`dark matter` repository and is what would read a real one.